    title:"The Philosophy of Self",
    duration:"15-20 min",
    hook:"Imagine that every cell in your body is replaced over time — most cells within 7-10 years. The atoms that make you up today are mostly not the atoms that made you up ten years ago. Your beliefs, values, personality, and memories have changed since childhood. So what exactly makes you the same person who existed ten years ago?",
    bigIdea:"The question of personal identity — what makes you the same person over time — has been debated for millennia without resolution, with different answers having profound implications for ethics, spirituality, and how you live.",
    sections:[
      {title:"Theories of Personal Identity",emoji:"🪞",content:"Physical continuity: you are your body. Problem: your body changes almost entirely over time. Psychological continuity (Locke): you are the same person because of memory and psychological connections. Problem: you cannot remember being an infant. Soul theory: you are the same person because of an immaterial, unchanging soul. Problem: no scientific evidence. Bundle theory (Hume): there is no self — only a bundle of perceptions with no underlying self holding them together. Buddhist no-self (anatta): the self is an illusion constructed by mind — there is no fixed, permanent self."},
      {title:"The Ship of Theseus",emoji:"🚢",content:"If the ship of Theseus has every plank replaced one by one until no original plank remains, is it still the same ship? Applied to persons: if your body and mind change so completely that nothing original remains, are you still the same person? Derek Parfit's teleportation thought experiment: you step into a teleporter that destroys your body and creates an exact copy on Mars. Is the person who arrives you? Parfit's conclusion: personal identity is not what matters — what matters is psychological continuity and connectedness, not whether some metaphysical same-person criterion is met."},
      {title:"Eastern Perspectives on Self",emoji:"🌸",content:"Buddhist anatta (no-self): what we call I is actually a collection of five aggregates (form, sensation, perception, mental formations, consciousness) with no fixed self underlying them. Advaita Vedanta (non-dualism): the individual self (atman) is identical to universal consciousness (Brahman) — the sense of being a separate self is maya (illusion). Taoism: the self that insists on itself creates suffering — wu wei means releasing the ego's compulsion to control. Zen: the koan What is your original face before your parents were born? points to awareness prior to personal identity."},
      {title:"Practical Implications",emoji:"💡",content:"The fixed self view: I am fundamentally this kind of person — leads to fixed mindset and defensiveness. The constructed self view: this is a story I am telling about myself — opens possibility for conscious revision. The no-self view: there is no fixed self to defend — can reduce reactivity and the suffering that comes from protecting a self-image. Compassion: if you understand your self as constructed and contingent — the product of genetics, upbringing, culture — it becomes easier to extend the same understanding to others."}
    ],
    activity:{title:"Self Investigation",instructions:"Write responses to: (1) Who were you at age 5? In what ways is that person you? (2) Which is more you — your body, your memories, your personality, or something else? (3) If you completely changed your personality, beliefs, and memories through an experience, would you still be you? (4) What story do you tell about yourself that you know is not quite accurate but keep telling anyway? (5) If you had to describe yourself without using your name, job, relationships, history, or physical description — what would remain?"},
