    sections: [
      {
        title: "What Intention Setting Actually Does",
        emoji: "🎯",
        content: "Setting clear intentions activates several well-documented psychological mechanisms. The Reticular Activating System (RAS): the brain's attention filter — it determines what sensory information reaches conscious awareness. When you set a clear intention (I want to find a great opportunity to help someone today), the RAS begins filtering for evidence of that intention, making relevant information more salient. This is why you notice a particular car everywhere after deciding to buy one — the car was always there; your attention filter changed. Implementation intentions (Gollwitzer research): 'When X situation occurs, I will do Y' — specific intentions dramatically increase behavior follow-through. Goal setting: the extensive research on goal setting shows that specific, written goals are significantly more likely to be achieved than vague aspirations — the act of clarifying and committing activates consistent attention and action."
      },
      {
        title: "The Evidence for Mental Rehearsal",
        emoji: "🏆",
        content: "Beyond goal setting, the mental rehearsal research is relevant to intention setting. As covered in the Joe Dispenza lesson: athletes who mentally rehearse with vivid detail and emotional engagement show nearly as much motor cortex activation and skill improvement as those physically practicing. The mechanism: the brain changes in response to vivid mental experience with similar mechanisms to physical experience. Effective mental rehearsal includes: specific sensory detail (what do you see, hear, feel?), emotional engagement (feel the emotion of achieving the intention, not just visualizing the outcome), process as well as outcome (rehearse the steps, not just the destination), and regular repetition. This is not magical thinking — it is documented neuroplasticity applied to intentional practice."
      },
      {
        title: "The Manifestation Question",
        emoji: "⚖️",
        content: "The 'law of attraction' and manifestation claims — that thoughts literally attract corresponding physical reality through quantum or metaphysical mechanisms — are significantly more speculative than the psychological effects described above. What is well-supported: intentions shape attention, attention shapes action, action shapes outcomes. Clear intentions also affect others' behavior (people who project confidence and clarity genuinely receive different responses). The placebo effect shows that belief can alter physiology. What is more speculative: the claim that intentions directly alter physical reality without the mediation of action, attention, and behavior. The critique of naïve manifestation teaching: it can produce magical thinking that replaces action with visualization, blames victims for negative outcomes ('you attracted this'), and ignores structural factors (poverty, discrimination, health) that significantly constrain individual outcomes regardless of intention. The skillful position: use the psychological mechanisms that are documented; remain skeptical of metaphysical claims."
      },
      {
        title: "A Practical Intention Practice",
        emoji: "🌟",
        content: "Effective intention setting combines clarity, emotion, and action. The practice: Morning intention: spend 5 minutes each morning clarifying your intention for the day. Not 'I want to be productive' but 'I intend to be fully present in each conversation I have today.' Feel the quality you are intending — bring it alive emotionally, not just conceptually. Throughout the day: when you lose the intention (inevitable), notice and return. Review each evening: did you live the intention? What got in the way? What supported it? The deeper practice: set intentions not just for daily behavior but for who you are becoming. 'I am becoming someone who responds to difficulty with curiosity rather than fear.' 'I am becoming someone who brings genuine presence to everyone I encounter.' Identity-level intentions activate the most powerful behavior change mechanisms."
      }
    ],
